WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.570
Narrator: It's like they
have magical powers.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.730
When the weather warms,
fruit flies just appear.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.160
Out of nowhere.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.140
Next to the fruit bowl,
near the trash, everywhere.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.870
And once they arrive, they
are determined to stay

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.630
no matter how many you
swat, squash, or seize.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.290
Now as far as we know,
fruit flies can't teleport

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.840
into our homes from
another grosser dimension.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.140
So where do they come from,
and why are they so hard

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:27.973
to get rid of?

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.400
Meet Drosophila melanogaster:

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.600
your standard fruit fly.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.220
It can smell a meal from
more than a kilometer away

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.720
all thanks to the tiny
antennae on top of its head.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.830
These antennae are specially attuned to

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.410
sniff out chemicals like acetic acid

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.410
let off by rotting fruit,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.350
and once they're locked on to the scent,

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.640
it's nearly impossible to
keep them out of your home.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.350
That's because fruit flies are about

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.550
the size of a sesame seed,

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.670
so they can slip through almost any crack,

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.420
screen, or gap.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.470
But contrary to popular belief,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.460
it's not just the smelly
fruit they're after.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.060
They're also hunting for the
source of that rotting stench,

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.780
specifically, fungi and
other microorganisms.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.070
Yum!

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.730
And once they land for the
feast, things turn even nastier.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.270
You know how you never
see just one fruit fly?

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.160
That's because they have
incredibly fast life cycles.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.720
A single female fruit fly can

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.720
lay up to 100 eggs a day, which
hatch in less than 24 hours.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.630
The maggots then tunnel
under the fruit skin,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.453
feeding on the microbial rot.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.790
In just a few days later, they pupate

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.520
into fully fledged fruit flies.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.800
By the time day 11 or 12 rolls around,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.082
they're ready to have
little maggots of their own.

00:01:52.082 --> 00:01:53.100
Aww.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.700
That's why your home can
go from fruit-fly free

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.770
to infested in under two weeks.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.730
Now, by this point you might start

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.010
swatting them down one by one.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.990
But it's not so easy, right?

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.650
Perhaps even...fruitless?

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.280
Well, scientists have discovered why.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.410
Turns out fruit flies are
mathematical wizards of escape.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.310
For starters, they have
around 270-degree vision,

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.600
so they can see you coming
from almost any angle:

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.300
front, back, or side to side,

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.120
and they will actually calculate the angle

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.410
of your attack and plan
their escape accordingly

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.920
all in as little as 100 milliseconds.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.540
Scientists figured this out because

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.960
of how a resting fruit fly will reposition

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.860
its legs when it senses an attack.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.780
If your hand's coming from
the front, for example,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.080
fruit flies move their
middle legs forward,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.990
lean back, and raise
their legs, allowing for

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.120
a fast backwards takeoff.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.470
Once in midair, killing
them isn't much easier.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.140
They can change directions within

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.570
one one-hundredth of a
second and quickly accelerate

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.940
by flapping their wings
200 times per second.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.070
Not bad for a brain that's even smaller

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.950
than a house fly's.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.253
So then, how do you get rid of them?

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.640
Some experts suggest making a trap.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.050
Fill a container with 2
centimeters of cider vinegar,

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.600
cap it with a funnel, and
then tape around the perimeter

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.080
so that no flies can crawl out.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.310
You see, fruit flies are
smart enough to find fruit

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.580
but not the hole they entered.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.910
But here's the thing.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.450
Even if you trap every last fly,

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.310
they will come back as long
as there's something to eat.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.280
And unfortunately for you,
fruit flies aren't picky.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.430
They love rotten fruit, sure,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.710
but wine and other fermented liquids,

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.400
which are chock-full of
those microbes they adore,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.120
are also tasty,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.660
as is the slime that builds
up in your kitchen sink.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.800
So really, the only thing you can do

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.040
to get rid of them is to,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.953
well, clean up.

